      "text": "Since the cost of a pencil is constant, we can define it as \\( p \\) and the cost of a pen as \\( q \\). We have the following system of linear equations based on the given information:\n\n\\[\n4q + 3p = 224 \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\\[\n2q + 5p = 154 \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate one of the variables, we can use the method of elimination. First, we will eliminate \\( q \\) by making the coefficients of \\( q \\) in both equations the same. We can do this by multiplying equation (1) by 2 and equation (2) by 4:\n\n\\[\n2(4q + 3p) = 2(224) \\implies 8q + 6p = 448 \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\\[\n4(2q + 5p) = 4(154) \\implies 8q + 20p = 616 \\quad \\text{(4)}\n\\]\n\nNext, we subtract equation (3) from equation (4):\n\n\\[\n(8q + 20p) - (8q + 6p) = 616 - 448\n\\]\n\\[\n8q + 20p - 8q - 6p = 168\n\\]\n\\[\n14p = 168\n\\]\n\\[\np = \\frac{168}{14} = 12\n\\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pencil is \\( p = 12 \\) dollars, which is 1200 cents. Now we substitute \\( p = 12 \\) back into equation (1) to find \\( q \\):\n\n\\[\n4q + 3(12) = 224\n\\]\n\\[\n4q + 36 = 224\n\\]\n\\[\n4q = 188\n\\]\n\\[\nq = \\frac{188}{4} = 47\n\\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pen is \\( q = 47 \\) dollars, or 4700 cents. The cost of a pencil is \\( p = 1200 \\) cents. Therefore, the cost of a pencil is \\(\\boxed{1200}\\) cents.",
