      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we can set up a system of linear equations based on the given information. Let's denote the cost of one pen by \\( P \\) and the cost of one pencil by \\( C \\). \n\nFrom the problem, we have the following equations:\n1. \\( 4P + 3C = 2.24 \\)\n2. \\( 2P + 5C = 1.54 \\)\n\nFirst, we will eliminate one of the variables by manipulating these equations. Let's eliminate \\( P \\) first. To do this, we will multiply the second equation by 2 to make the coefficient of \\( P \\) in both equations the same:\n\\[ 2(2P + 5C) = 2(1.54) \\]\n\\[ 4P + 10C = 3.08 \\]\n\nNow we have the modified system of equations:\n1. \\( 4P + 3C = 2.24 \\)\n2. \\( 4P + 10C = 3.08 \\)\n\nNext, we subtract the first equation from the second equation to eliminate \\( P \\):\n\\[ (4P + 10C) - (4P + 3C) = 3.08 - 2.24 \\]\n\\[ 4P + 10C - 4P - 3C = 0.84 \\]\n\\[ 7C = 0.84 \\]\n\nNow, we solve for \\( C \\) by dividing both sides of the equation by 7:\n\\[ C = \\frac{0.84}{7} \\]\n\\[ C = 0.12 \\]\n\nSo, the cost of a pencil is \\( 0.12 \\) dollars. To express this in cents, we convert dollars to cents by multiplying by 100:\n\\[ 0.12 \\times 100 = 12 \\]\n\nTherefore, the cost of a pencil is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) cents.",
